/// A United States citizen living abroad.
///
/// The United States taxes its citizens on everything they earn anywhere, so
/// nothing of `us` stops applying when someone moves. A citizen abroad `lives us`
/// (or a state of it, until the move) and, on the days abroad, also
/// `lives us/abroad` and the country. Residences may overlap, so both systems
/// govern the same days. This one adds what only a citizen abroad has:
///
///   foreign-earned   pay from a place of kind `foreign-wages`, in dollars
///   feie-excluded    the foreign earned income exclusion (Form 2555)
///
/// The exclusion is an adjustment: it counts into the `adjustments` line of `us`,
/// which takes it off income, and the tax it costs through the "stacking rule"
/// counts into `other-taxes`, so the payments already made are set against the
/// whole of it.
///
/// Simplified on purpose: the choice between the exclusion and the foreign tax
/// credit is fixed, and a year's qualifying days are a param (laws cannot count
/// days). The housing exclusion is not modelled.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS (Rev.
/// Proc. 2024-40 for 2025) before relying on them.
system us/abroad

use us


/// Pay earned abroad, from an employer in the country of residence.
kind foreign-wages : wages


/// Pay from a foreign employer counts as foreign earned income, in dollars at
/// the rate of the day.
///
/// To fix: pay earned abroad must arrive from a place of kind `foreign-wages`.
law count-foreign-earned
  on in
  when from is foreign-wages
  count value(amount, USD) as foreign-earned

/// The foreign earned income exclusion (IRC §911): pay earned abroad, up to a
/// yearly limit prorated by the days spent abroad, is not taxed by the United
/// States. It comes off income as an adjustment. The income that stays is taxed
/// as if the excluded pay were on top of it (IRC §911(f), the "stacking rule"): the
/// extra tax is the tax on everything less the tax on the excluded pay less the
/// tax on what is left, and it is added to the year's other taxes.
///
/// To fix: `days-abroad` is the number of qualifying days in the year; the
/// exclusion cannot exceed the limit for those days or the pay earned abroad.
law feie
  each year closing 04-15
  let cap = feie-limit[year] * days-abroad[year] / 365
  let excluded = min(tally(foreign-earned), cap)
  let with-it = max(tally(total-income) - tally(adjustments) - standard-deduction[year, owner.filing], empty)
  let without = max(with-it - excluded, empty)
  let stacking = progressive(ordinary[year, owner.filing], with-it) - progressive(ordinary[year, owner.filing], excluded) - progressive(ordinary[year, owner.filing], without)
  count excluded as feie-excluded
  count excluded as adjustments
  count max(stacking, empty) as other-taxes
  count max(stacking, empty) as feie-stacking-tax


// The exclusion limit per person (IRC §911(b)(2)(D)). Rev. Proc. 2024-40 (2025).
// verify: the 2026 figure against the next Rev. Proc.
param feie-limit
  2025 130_000 USD
  2026 132_900 USD

// Qualifying days abroad in the year: 2025-07-01 to 2025-12-31 is 184 days.
param days-abroad
  2025 184
  2026 365
